Reason:
After handing a complete frame to `pn533_recv_frame()`, the original code attempts to allocate a new receive buffer. If this allocation fails, the function returns 0 even though bytes from the input have already been consumed (the loop variable `i` has advanced). This violates the `receive_buf()` accounting contract expected by the serdev core and leaves `dev->recv_skb` as NULL. On the next invocation, `skb_put_u8()` dereferences the NULL pointer, causing a crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
        if (!dev->recv_skb) {
            dev->recv_skb = alloc_skb(PN532_UART_SKB_BUFF_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
            if (!dev->recv_skb)
                return i;
        }
</text>
and
<text>
        pn533_recv_frame(dev->priv, dev->recv_skb, 0);
        dev->recv_skb = NULL;
</text>
Reason:
The fix moves the allocation of the receive skb to occur lazily before each byte is consumed, ensuring the buffer is always valid before writing into it. If the allocation fails, the function returns the number of bytes already accepted (`i`), which correctly accounts for consumed bytes and avoids violating the serdev contract. After a complete frame is handed off, `dev->recv_skb` is set to NULL instead of being immediately re‑allocated, so the next loop iteration will attempt a fresh allocation. This eliminates the NULL pointer dereference and maintains proper accounting.

